# Andela Time Tracking

The user tracks progress against a 4–6h MVP target (16h ceiling) and asks for an
"Elapsed Time" report. Get this right — it has been reported wrong twice.

## The one rule

**Cumulative elapsed = `current UTC − start UTC`. One subtraction. Always.**

- **start UTC** = the first real working prompt in `docs/prompts.md`:
  `2026-06-18T08:04:59Z` (= 1:35 PM IST). Ignore the earlier `bootstrap` entry.
- **current UTC** = run `date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ"` **at the moment you write
  the report**, in the same turn, as the LAST step before composing the answer.
  This is the ONLY authoritative source for "now".

### Why NOT the `<timestamp>` tag

The `<timestamp>` tag is the time the user **hit send** — it is captured before
the turn runs. A turn can take many minutes (tools, edits, tests), so by the time
the report is printed the tag is already stale (e.g. tag says 3:56 PM but the wall
clock is 4:11 PM). **Never report elapsed time from the `<timestamp>` tag.** It may
only be used as a rough lower bound for a sanity check, never as "now".

`docs/prompts.md` is appended automatically by the `beforeSubmitPrompt` hook, so
it is the source of truth for prompt (start) times.

## Never estimate the current time

There are two recurring failures:
1. **Inventing/extrapolating** the current time (e.g. writing "≈ 4:06 PM" when it
   was 3:46 PM).
2. **Reusing the stale `<timestamp>` tag** instead of reading the wall clock at
   report time (e.g. reporting 3:56 PM when the turn finished at 4:11 PM).

The current time is NOT something you reason about or copy from the prompt context
— it is read verbatim from `date -u`, run in the same turn you report.

- ❌ Do not add "thinking/working time" to the last known time.
- ❌ Do not round up "to be safe".
- ❌ Do not report "now" from the `<timestamp>` tag.
- ✅ Run `date -u` as the last step before reporting, subtract `start`, and quote
  the raw `date -u` output you used.

## Never do this

- ❌ Do **not** sum per-phase estimates to produce a cumulative. Rounding and
  thinking/tool overhead inflate each phase, so the sum exceeds wall-clock —
  which is impossible.
- ❌ Do **not** eyeball or estimate from memory.

## Per-phase figure (optional)

A single phase's duration may be `(this phase's prompt ts) − (previous phase's
prompt ts)`, both from `docs/prompts.md`. But the **cumulative** is still only the
single subtraction from `start`.

## Sanity check before reporting

`cumulative ≤ (now − start)` must hold. If your number is larger, it is wrong —
recompute with the single subtraction.
